==============================================================================
PART 1 — THE QUANTIFIER: 'at the attachment of a DEEPEST hair' IS VACUOUS
==============================================================================
  A single hair is trivially the deepest hair, and a hair may be hung at ANY vertex of
  G'.  So over the class of G' to which (F11-AT-w) is meant to apply, the prescribed w
  ranges over EVERY vertex.  (F11-AT-w) is therefore the UNIVERSAL statement

      (F11-ALL)   endpath(G', w) >= rad(G') + 4   for EVERY vertex w of G',

  and F11 = G42 is the EXISTENTIAL one.  (F11-AT-w) is STRICTLY STRONGER than F11, not a
  mild anchoring of it.  That is a defect in the r31 proposal and it is this round's first
  finding against its own previous entry.
  PG(2,3)            n=26   Sigma a = 104    l = 4.0000   ->  G'+pendant: Sigma a = 106, n = 27,
                     l(G) > 4  <==>  Sigma_{G'}(a-4) > 2   (exact threshold, not an estimate)
  Heawood            n=14   Sigma a = 42     l = 3.0000   ->  G'+pendant: Sigma a = 44, n = 15,
                     l(G) > 4  <==>  Sigma_{G'}(a-4) > 2   (exact threshold, not an estimate)
  blob_chain(3,5)    n=57   Sigma a = 220    l = 3.8596   ->  G'+pendant: Sigma a = 222, n = 58,
                     l(G) > 4  <==>  Sigma_{G'}(a-4) > 2   (exact threshold, not an estimate)
  CONTROL (pinned, not inferred): l(PG(2,3)) = 4.0 EXACTLY  [Sigma(a-4) = 0];
    PG(2,3) + one pendant has Sigma(a-4) = -2, i.e. l drops BELOW 4 — so the
    l-threshold on G is  Sigma_{G'}(a-4) > 2,  marginally stronger than l(G') > 4.

==============================================================================
PART 2 — (TAIL-1): C4-free and mu(G) >= 2  ==>  endpath(G,w) >= ecc(w) + 2, EVERY w
==============================================================================
  PROOF.  Let w = u_0 ... u_d be a geodesic to a vertex at distance d = ecc(w).  Since
  a(u_d) >= 2, G[N(u_d)] (a matching, because G is C4-free) has a component other than
  u_{d-1}'s; take y in it.  Then y !~ u_{d-1} by choice; y !~ u_i for i <= d-3 because
  dist(u_i,u_d) >= 3; and y !~ u_{d-2}, because otherwise u_{d-2} and u_d would have the
  TWO common neighbours u_{d-1} and y — a C4.  So u_0..u_d y is an induced path on d+2
  vertices with w as an ENDPOINT.  []
  Certified below by BUILDING the path at every vertex of every test graph and verifying
  it is induced — the construction is executed, not the conclusion asserted.
  C9                 n=9    rad=4  mu=2 : TAIL-1 built and CERTIFIED INDUCED at all 9 vertices, always ecc(w)+2
  C11                n=11   rad=5  mu=2 : TAIL-1 built and CERTIFIED INDUCED at all 11 vertices, always ecc(w)+2
  2xC9 glued         n=17   rad=4  mu=2 : TAIL-1 built and CERTIFIED INDUCED at all 17 vertices, always ecc(w)+2
  2xC5 glued         n=9    rad=2  mu=2 : TAIL-1 built and CERTIFIED INDUCED at all 9 vertices, always ecc(w)+2
  Petersen           n=10   rad=2  mu=3 : TAIL-1 built and CERTIFIED INDUCED at all 10 vertices, always ecc(w)+2
  Heawood            n=14   rad=3  mu=3 : TAIL-1 built and CERTIFIED INDUCED at all 14 vertices, always ecc(w)+2
  PG(2,3)            n=26   rad=3  mu=4 : TAIL-1 built and CERTIFIED INDUCED at all 26 vertices, always ecc(w)+2
  blob_chain(2,5)    n=33   rad=6  mu=2 : TAIL-1 built and CERTIFIED INDUCED at all 33 vertices, always ecc(w)+2
  blob_chain(3,5)    n=57   rad=6  mu=2 : TAIL-1 built and CERTIFIED INDUCED at all 57 vertices, always ecc(w)+2
  TOTAL: 186 (graph, vertex) pairs, every one carrying an explicit induced witness.
  TIGHTNESS (hardest form): on C6, endpath(w) = ecc(w)+2 = 5 EXACTLY for every w —
    so TAIL-1 cannot be improved to ecc(w)+3 without a further hypothesis.
  LIVENESS, CORRECTLY AIMED: on C9+pendant (mu = 1), the 2 vertices whose FURTHEST
    vertex is the a=1 pendant make the construction DECLINE 2/2 times.
    The mis-aimed version (probe AT the pendant) SUCCEEDS and tests nothing — kept as
    a named negative, because a probe that cannot fail scores ABSENT, not PASS.

==============================================================================
PART 3 — (TAIL-2) COSTS a(y) >= 4, AND (TAIL-3) COSTS a(z) >= 6
==============================================================================
  SECOND EXTENSION.  Continue u_0..u_d y by z in N(y).  Forced for free by C4-freeness:
  z !~ u_d (choose z off u_d's component of G[N(y)]) and z !~ u_{d-1} (else z-y-u_d-u_{d-1}
  is a 4-cycle).  z !~ u_i for i <= d-4 because dist(u_i,y) >= 3.  What must be PAID FOR is
  exactly u_{d-2} and u_{d-3}: at most ONE vertex of N(y) is adjacent to each of them (two
  would give y and that u_j two common neighbours — a C4), so at most two components of the
  matching G[N(y)] are spoiled, plus u_d's.  Hence  a(y) >= 4  suffices.

  THIRD EXTENSION.  Continue by z' in N(z).  z' !~ u_d is forced (z'-z-y-u_d is a 4-cycle).
  The dodge list is then {u_{d-1}, u_{d-2}, u_{d-3}, u_{d-4}} — FOUR vertices, each costing
  at most one component — plus y's component.  Hence  a(z) >= 6  suffices, and nothing
  smaller is delivered by this argument.

  >>> l(G) > 4 IS A MEAN.  It does not supply a(y) >= 4, let alone a(z) >= 6, AT A
  >>> PRESCRIBED VERTEX.  This is the DENSITY-vs-POSITIONAL mismatch, now LOCATED at a
  >>> named vertex and carrying a NUMBER.
  Petersen           n=10   : TAIL-2 succeeded at  10/ 10 vertices; a(y) >= 4 held at   0 of those
  Heawood            n=14   : TAIL-2 succeeded at  14/ 14 vertices; a(y) >= 4 held at   0 of those
  PG(2,3)            n=26   : TAIL-2 succeeded at  26/ 26 vertices; a(y) >= 4 held at  26 of those
  blob_chain(2,5)    n=33   : TAIL-2 succeeded at  33/ 33 vertices; a(y) >= 4 held at   0 of those
  blob_chain(3,5)    n=57   : TAIL-2 succeeded at  57/ 57 vertices; a(y) >= 4 held at  57 of those
  2xC9 glued         n=17   : TAIL-2 succeeded at  17/ 17 vertices; a(y) >= 4 held at   0 of those
  C4-FORCED exclusions verified on 157 frames: y !~ u_{d-1} and y !~ u_{d-2} every time.
  NULL EXPECTATION stated: y !~ u_{d-2} is NOT automatic for an arbitrary neighbour of
    u_d — it is exactly what C4-freeness buys, and a C4-carrying host would break it.

==============================================================================
PART 4 — THE THEOREM: route A2's conclusion for the SINGLE-HAIR case,
         plus (RAD-1P), the radius dichotomy the machine forced me to find
==============================================================================
  SETTING.  G connected C4-free; G' = peel(G) connected with mu(G') >= 2; the peeled set
  is a SINGLE hair of h >= 1 vertices hung at w in V(G').  (This is exactly the class in
  which r31 killed (A2-PATH): its witness was G' + ONE pendant.)  Write r := rad(G') and
  e := ecc_{G'}(w).

  TWO BOUNDS.
    (B1)  G' is INDUCED in G, so path(G) >= path(G').  With F11 = G42 on G' this reads
          path(G) >= r + 4.       [needs G' to satisfy F11: C4-free, l>4, rad>=5, mu>=2 —
                                   the SAME import route A2 has always made, inherited here]
    (B2)  The hair prepends h vertices to any induced path of G' ending at w, so
          path(G) >= h + endpath(G',w) >= h + e + 2.        [TAIL-1, UNCONDITIONAL]

  RADIUS, single hair at w:  ecc_G(w) = max(e, h), so rad(G) <= max(e, h); and rad(G) <= r+h.

  CASE ANALYSIS.
    h >= 2 :  e >= h  ->  rad(G) <= e  and (B2) gives h+e+2 >= e+4.            CLOSED
              h  > e  ->  rad(G) <= h  and (B2) gives h+e+2 >= h+4 (e >= 2).   CLOSED
              -- UNCONDITIONAL: no l > 4, no rad >= 5, no F11, no (F11-AT-w).
    h = 1  :  rad(G) <= min(e, r+1).
              e = r     : rad(G) <= r, (B1) gives r+4 >= rad(G)+4.             CLOSED (via F11)
              e >= r+2  : rad(G) <= r+1, (B2) gives e+3 >= r+5 >= rad(G)+4.    CLOSED
              e = r+1   : (B1) gives r+4, (B2) gives r+4, and rad(G)+4 is r+5 if and only if
                          rad(G) = r+1.                    SHORT BY EXACTLY ONE VERTEX.

  (RAD-1P) — THE RADIUS DICHOTOMY FOR ONE PENDANT.  For c in V(G'),
  ecc_G(c) = max(ecc_{G'}(c), d(c,w)+1), and ecc_G(pendant) = e+1.  Hence
        rad(G) = r      <==>  SOME centre c of G' has d(c,w) <= r-1,
        rad(G) = r + 1  <==>  EVERY centre c of G' has d(c,w) = r   (and never more).
  So the h = 1, e = r+1 case is only residual when w is ALSO at maximum distance from every
  centre.  I did not have this condition when I wrote the case analysis; the machine
  produced an EMPTY residual class and that is how the condition was found.
  CASE TALLY over 7404 (base, w, h) instances from 145 bases, every one machine-checked:
     h>=2 CLOSED by (B2)                      4936
     h=1 e=r CLOSED by (B1)                    908
     h=1 e>=r+2 CLOSED by (B2)                 723
     h=1 e=r+1, rad(G)=r  CLOSED by (B1)       835
     h=1 e=r+1, rad(G)=r+1  RESIDUAL             2
  (RAD-1P) verified as an EQUIVALENCE on every h=1 instance; it FIRED (rad(G)=r+1)
     on 34 of them, so the dichotomy's non-trivial side is LIVE, not vacuous.
  THE CONCLUSION, computed EXACTLY (longest induced path) on every host with n <= 15,
  SPLIT BY WHICH BOUND CARRIES IT — the two branches do NOT have the same status:
     h >= 2, closed by (B2) ALONE (UNCONDITIONAL — asserted): 244/244 hold, 0 violations
     h  = 1, carried by (B1) = F11 on G' (IMPORT ABSENT on these sparse hosts):
            117 hold, 5 FAIL — and the failures are the point:
            C5                 w=0  path(G)=5  < rad(G)+4=6    l(G')=2.000  rad(G')=2
            C5                 w=2  path(G)=5  < rad(G)+4=6    l(G')=2.000  rad(G')=2
            C6                 w=0  path(G)=6  < rad(G)+4=7    l(G')=2.000  rad(G')=3
            C6                 w=3  path(G)=6  < rad(G)+4=7    l(G')=2.000  rad(G')=3
            Theta(3,3,3)       w=0  path(G)=6  < rad(G)+4=7    l(G')=2.250  rad(G')=3
     >>> HARDEST-FORM LIVENESS CONTROL, and it was NOT designed — the machine produced it
     >>> when a check of mine ran on a sample outside its own hypothesis (own defect, PART 7).
     >>> DELETE F11 AND THE h = 1 BRANCH IS FALSE.  C5 + one pendant has path = 5 and
     >>> rad + 4 = 6.  So (B1) is not decorative bookkeeping in that branch: it is the whole
     >>> of it, and the h = 1 branch is exactly as strong as F11's applicability to G'.
     >>> The h >= 2 branch imports nothing and holds on every one of these same hosts.
     366 exact longest-induced-path computations in total.

==============================================================================
PART 5 — THE RESIDUAL HUNT.  POPULATION FIRST, VERDICT SECOND.
==============================================================================
  The residual of PART 4 is a CONJUNCTION of three conditions:
      (a) h = 1;   (b) ecc_{G'}(w) = rad(G') + 1;   (c) every centre of G' has d(c,w) = rad(G').
  (b) says w is nearly peripheral in ECCENTRICITY; (c) says w is maximally far from the
  CENTRE.  They pull in the same direction and yet the sweep finds them never co-occurring.
  POPULATIONS over 145 bases:
     (b) ecc(w) = rad+1                       :   837 vertices
     (c) w maximally far from EVERY centre    :    34 vertices
     (b) AND (c)  = the residual              :     2 vertices
  >>> THE RESIDUAL IS NON-EMPTY.  My FIRST, narrower family produced ZERO of them
  >>> and I was one sentence away from recording the single-hair case as closed
  >>> outright.  The SEEDED RANDOM HOSTS produced them.  A residual that only a
  >>> wider family exhibits is exactly the residual a narrow family lets you
  >>> claim away — so the width of the family is load-bearing, not decoration.
  RESIDUAL INSTANCES FOUND (2), each MEASURED rather than merely listed:
    base                 w    n    ecc(w)   rad(G')  l(G')   TAIL-2?  endpath(w) vs rad+4
    rand(s=7,n0=18)      11   18   5        4        2.333   YES      12 vs 8
    rand(s=30,n0=22)     13   22   5        4        2.455   no       17 vs 8
  (TAIL-2) discharges 1 of 2 residual instances BY CONSTRUCTION.
  >>> AND READ THE endpath COLUMN: on these hosts endpath(w) is already at or past
  >>> rad(G')+4.  The residual is a GAP IN THE ARGUMENT, not a counterexample to
  >>> anything.  The BOUND is short by one vertex; the GRAPHS are not.
  EVERY residual instance carries l(G') <= 4 : the l > 4 hypothesis is UNSPENT
     on all of them, exactly as r31 measured for (A2-PATH) over 179 witnesses.
  NULL EXPECTATION, stated: condition (c) alone occurs at 1.4% of swept vertices and
     condition (b) at 33.9%; if they were INDEPENDENT the expected joint count over
     2468 vertices would be 11.53.  So the empty conjunction carries only about that
     much information — it is a HINT, and it is reported as one.

